select 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In supervised learning, the training data consists of...","options":["inputs only, with no labels","input-output pairs (x, y)","rewards from an environment","a single labeled example"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Supervised learning is given examples (x, y) and must learn the map from x to y - see ''The learning problem''."},{"prompt":"E_in is the error a hypothesis makes on...","options":["the training sample","the whole unseen population","the test set only","a single point"],"answer":0,"explanation":"E_in (in-sample error) is measured on the data you trained on; E_out is on everything else."},{"prompt":"The perceptron is a model that separates two classes with...","options":["a curved boundary","a straight line (a hyperplane)","a decision tree","no boundary at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A perceptron classifies by the sign of w^T x, a linear boundary - see ''The perceptron and linear models''."},{"prompt":"Overfitting means a model...","options":["fits training data poorly","fits the training data well but generalizes badly","has too few parameters","cannot reduce E_in"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An overfit model drives E_in down while E_out stays high - it memorized noise."},{"prompt":"As you make a model more complex, its variance across different training sets typically...","options":["decreases","stays fixed","increases","becomes zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"More complex models are more sensitive to which sample they saw, so variance rises - see ''The bias-variance tradeoff''."},{"prompt":"Regularization is used mainly to...","options":["lower E_in as much as possible","constrain the model to fight overfitting","speed up training","add more features"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Regularization penalizes large or complex fits so the model generalizes better."},{"prompt":"A validation set is data you use to...","options":["train the final weights","estimate E_out and choose between models","increase the sample size","define the labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Validation gives an honest estimate of out-of-sample error for model selection - see ''Validation and cross-validation''."},{"prompt":"Gradient descent updates a weight vector by moving...","options":["toward the gradient","opposite the gradient of the error","randomly","to the average of the data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You step in the negative gradient direction to reduce the error - see ''Gradient descent and logistic regression''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The learning problem: what are we even doing?', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Frame a task as a learning problem, name its pieces, and tell supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement setups apart. Warm-up: a bank wants to predict whether a new applicant will repay a loan, using records of past applicants and whether they repaid. Which of the three setups is that?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Learning from data means using examples to approximate an unknown target function f that maps an input x to an output y. You never see f directly; you see a finite sample of pairs (x_1, y_1), ..., (x_N, y_N) drawn from some distribution. A learning algorithm searches a hypothesis set H and returns one hypothesis g that it hopes is close to f. The three classic setups differ in what the data gives you. Supervised: each x comes with its correct label y, and you learn the map. Unsupervised: you get inputs x with no labels and must find structure, such as clusters. Reinforcement: you get no correct answer, only a reward signal after each action, and must learn which actions pay off. The whole game is choosing g from H so that g agrees with f not just on the sample but on new inputs."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Loan approval: inputs are applicant features (income, debt, history), the label is repaid or defaulted, and past records are the sample - supervised. Grouping shoppers by buying habits with no predefined categories - unsupervised. A program learning to play a board game only from win or loss at the end - reinforcement. Size the pieces: if each input x has 5 features and the label is +1 or -1, then f maps a 5-dimensional vector to a sign, and a linear H would pick weights w so that g(x) = sign(w^T x). A tiny sample might be N = 4 points; the danger, which the rest of the course studies, is that agreeing with f on 4 points says little about the millions of unseen inputs."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Recommending films from a user''s past star ratings of films - which setup? 2) Detecting unusual login patterns with no examples of ''bad'' labeled in advance - which setup? 3) A robot learning to walk from a reward for distance traveled - which setup? 4) In supervised learning, what does the target f map from and to? 5) What is the difference between g and f? Answers: 1) supervised (ratings are labels). 2) unsupervised (no labels; you seek structure or outliers). 3) reinforcement (only a reward, no correct action given). 4) from an input x to an output y. 5) f is the true, unknown target; g is the hypothesis the algorithm returns to approximate it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling a task unsupervised just because it feels hard - if labels exist in the data, it is supervised. Mistake: confusing H, the set of candidate hypotheses, with g, the single one you pick. Exit: (a) Which setup learns only from a reward signal? (b) What three symbols describe the target, the chosen hypothesis, and the hypothesis set? Answers: reinforcement; f, g, and H."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep three symbols straight: f is the unknown true target, g is the hypothesis your algorithm returns, and H is the whole set of hypotheses it may choose from.', 13),
  (3, 'Can we learn? In-sample versus out-of-sample', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why fitting the sample is not enough, and use a bin-of-marbles argument to see when in-sample error predicts out-of-sample error. Warm-up: you draw 10 marbles from a huge bin and 3 are red. What is your best single guess for the fraction of red marbles in the whole bin?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Split error into two numbers. E_in(g) is the fraction of the training sample that g gets wrong. E_out(g) is the probability that g is wrong on a fresh input drawn from the same distribution. Learning means making E_out small, but you only ever see E_in. The bridge is a probability fact. Imagine a bin with an unknown fraction mu of red marbles; draw a sample of size N and let nu be the sample fraction of red. Hoeffding''s inequality says the chance that nu differs from mu by more than a tolerance eps is at most 2 exp(-2 eps^2 N). In learning, a fixed hypothesis is one bin: mu is E_out, nu is E_in. So for a single fixed h, E_in is probably close to E_out once N is large. The catch: a learning algorithm tries many hypotheses and picks the best-looking one, so the guarantee must be paid for across all of H - that is the next lessons'' subject."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Bin: draw N = 100, observe nu = 0.3 red. Hoeffding with eps = 0.1 bounds the chance that the true mu is off by more than 0.1 at 2 exp(-2 * 0.01 * 100) = 2 exp(-2), about 0.27 - not tight yet. Push to N = 1000: 2 exp(-2 * 0.01 * 1000) = 2 exp(-20), about 4e-9 - now E_in pins E_out. For a single fixed classifier that gets 5 of 100 test-like draws wrong, E_in = 0.05 is a trustworthy estimate of E_out when N is large. But if you scan M = 1000 different hypotheses and report the smallest E_in, the honest bound multiplies the failure probability by M: 2M exp(-2 eps^2 N). More hypotheses means you need more data to trust the winner."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Define E_in in one sentence. 2) Define E_out in one sentence. 3) For a single fixed h, does large N make E_in close to E_out? 4) In the marble picture, what plays the role of E_out - mu or nu? 5) Why does choosing the best of many hypotheses weaken the Hoeffding guarantee? Answers: 1) the fraction of the training sample h classifies wrong. 2) the probability h is wrong on a fresh input from the distribution. 3) yes - Hoeffding makes the gap unlikely as N grows. 4) mu, the true bin fraction (E_in is nu). 5) the bad-event probability adds up over all the hypotheses you could have picked, so the union bound multiplies it by their count."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: celebrating a low E_in as if it were low E_out - a memorized sample can have E_in = 0 and terrible E_out. Mistake: applying the single-bin Hoeffding bound after searching many hypotheses without paying the union-bound price. Exit: (a) Which error can you actually measure? (b) What quantity must stay small for E_in to be a safe estimate after searching H? Answers: E_in; the effective number of hypotheses (the size or complexity of H)."}]}'::jsonb, 'E_in is error measured on the training sample; E_out is expected error on the whole distribution. Learning succeeds only when E_in is small AND E_in tracks E_out.', 14),
  (4, 'The perceptron and linear models', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Classify points with a perceptron, run its learning rule by hand, and see how one linear signal powers three different models. Warm-up: with weights w = (0, 1, 1) and input x = (1, 2, -3) (the leading 1 is the bias), is sign(w^T x) positive or negative?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A linear model scores an input by a weighted sum s = w^T x = w_0 + w_1 x_1 + ... + w_d x_d, where x_0 = 1 carries the bias. The perceptron outputs g(x) = sign(s): +1 on one side of the hyperplane w^T x = 0, -1 on the other. If the data are linearly separable, the Perceptron Learning Algorithm finds a separating line by repeating one fix: pick any misclassified point (x, y) and update w <- w + y x. That nudge rotates the boundary toward getting that point right, and a classic theorem guarantees the loop halts after finitely many updates when a separator exists. The same signal s drives two cousins: linear regression outputs s itself to fit a real number and has a one-shot least-squares solution, and logistic regression squashes s to a probability. One geometry, three jobs."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Signal: w = (0, 1, 1), x = (1, 2, -3) gives w^T x = 0*1 + 1*2 + 1*(-3) = -1, so sign is negative, class -1. PLA update: suppose w = (0, 0, 0) and a point x = (1, 2, 1) has true label y = +1. It is misclassified (signal 0 is not positive), so w <- w + y x = (1, 2, 1). Now w^T x = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6 > 0 - fixed. Capacity: in the plane, a line can shatter (label every way) some sets of 3 points but never 4, which is why the perceptron''s VC dimension in 2D is 3, foreshadowing the complexity lessons. Regression cousin: to fit y as a straight line through points, least squares chooses w minimizing the mean of (w^T x - y)^2, solvable in closed form."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With w = (0, 2, -1) and x = (1, 1, 3), compute w^T x and give the perceptron''s class. 2) In the PLA update w <- w + y x, what is y? 3) Does PLA halt when the data are not linearly separable? 4) Which linear model outputs the raw signal s as its answer? 5) How many points can a 2D perceptron shatter at most? Answers: 1) 0 + 2 - 3 = -1, class -1. 2) the true label of the misclassified point, +1 or -1. 3) no - it never converges without a separator, so you must cap iterations or use a variant. 4) linear regression. 5) 3."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting the bias term x_0 = 1, which forces every boundary through the origin. Mistake: running plain PLA on non-separable data and expecting it to stop. Exit: (a) What function does the perceptron apply to w^T x? (b) What single operation is the PLA update? Answers: the sign function; add y x to w for a misclassified point (x, y)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Write the linear signal as w^T x, a single number. For classification take its sign; for regression use it directly; for probability squash it through a logistic curve.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Learning from data succeeds only when E_in is small and also...","options":["E_out is even smaller than E_in","E_in is a good estimate of E_out","the sample size is exactly N = 30","the model has zero parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You need both a low training error and that it tracks the unseen error - see ''Can we learn?''."},{"prompt":"In the bin-of-marbles picture for a fixed hypothesis, the true out-of-sample error E_out corresponds to...","options":["the sample fraction nu","the bin fraction mu","the sample size N","the tolerance eps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"mu is the unknown true fraction (E_out); nu is what you observe in the sample (E_in)."},{"prompt":"The perceptron classifies an input x as...","options":["w^T x","sign(w^T x)","the logistic of w^T x","the average of x"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It outputs the sign of the linear signal, giving a hyperplane boundary."},{"prompt":"The Perceptron Learning Algorithm''s update on a misclassified point (x, y) is...","options":["w <- w - x","w <- w + y x","w <- y w","w <- x - w"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Add y x to nudge the boundary toward classifying that point correctly."},{"prompt":"Hoeffding''s bound 2 exp(-2 eps^2 N) tells you that as N grows, the chance E_in is far from E_out...","options":["grows","shrinks","stays constant","becomes exactly zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The exponential in N drives the bad-event probability down as the sample grows."},{"prompt":"Searching many hypotheses and reporting the best E_in is risky because...","options":["it makes E_in larger","the guarantee must be paid across all of H","it changes the target f","it removes the bias term"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The union bound multiplies the failure probability by the number of hypotheses considered."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Training versus testing and generalization', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Separate the training question (make E_in small) from the testing question (make E_out close to E_in), and read a generalization bound. Warm-up: a model scores E_in = 0.02 on training but E_out = 0.35 on fresh data. What is its generalization gap?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Learning has two separate demands. Training asks: can the algorithm drive E_in low on the sample? Testing, really generalization, asks: does that low E_in carry over, so E_out is close to E_in? A model can ace one and fail the other. The naive Hoeffding bound with M hypotheses, E_out <= E_in + sqrt((1/(2N)) ln(2M/delta)), is useless when H is infinite (M is unbounded). The fix is to replace the raw count M by a smarter measure of how many genuinely different labelings H can produce on N points, called the growth function. If H can label every one of the 2^N possible ways up to some size but no further, its power is limited, and the growth function grows only polynomially past that point. That polynomial, not the infinite M, sets the penalty - and its degree is the VC dimension of the next lesson."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Gap: E_out - E_in = 0.35 - 0.02 = 0.33 - a large gap signaling poor generalization despite great training error. Growth function: for a 2D perceptron, the number of distinct labelings it can realize on N points stops hitting the full 2^N at N = 4 and grows only like a cubic afterward, roughly (1/6)N^3 + ..., far below 2^N. Compare at N = 10: 2^10 = 1024 possible labelings, but a 2D perceptron realizes only on the order of 175 - it simply cannot express most patterns, and that limitation is exactly what makes it generalize. Bound intuition: with the penalty term shrinking like 1/sqrt(N), doubling from N = 250 to N = 1000 roughly halves the generalization penalty."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the generalization gap in symbols. 2) Which question does ''make E_in small'' belong to - training or testing? 3) Why is the M-hypotheses bound useless for a line in the plane? 4) What replaces M to rescue the bound? 5) If the penalty scales like 1/sqrt(N), what does quadrupling N do to it? Answers: 1) E_out - E_in. 2) training. 3) there are infinitely many lines, so M is unbounded and the bound blows up. 4) the growth function, which counts distinct labelings and grows only polynomially. 5) it halves the penalty (1/sqrt(4) = 1/2)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a model that expresses every pattern is best - unrestricted power destroys generalization. Mistake: using the finite-M bound for an infinite hypothesis set. Exit: (a) What are the two separate questions of learning? (b) What quantity replaces the raw hypothesis count in the bound? Answers: can we make E_in small, and does E_in track E_out; the growth function."}]}'::jsonb, 'The generalization gap is E_out minus E_in. A generalization bound says, with high probability, E_out is at most E_in plus a penalty that grows with model complexity and shrinks with N.', 14),
  (7, 'The VC dimension and model complexity', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define shattering and the VC dimension, compute it for simple models, and read the VC generalization bound. Warm-up: three non-collinear points in the plane - can a straight-line classifier produce all 8 of their plus/minus labelings?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A hypothesis set shatters a set of k points if, for every one of the 2^k ways to label them plus or minus, some hypothesis in H realizes exactly that labeling. The VC dimension d_vc is the largest k for which some placement of k points can be shattered. It is a single integer that captures the effective complexity of H. Two facts make it powerful. First, the growth function is bounded by a polynomial of degree d_vc, so d_vc controls generalization. Second, the VC bound states that with probability at least 1 - delta, E_out <= E_in + sqrt((8/N) ln(4 m_H(2N)/delta)), where the growth function m_H is at most O(N^d_vc). The penalty rises with d_vc and falls with N. A rule of thumb from this bound: you want roughly N of order 10 * d_vc examples to generalize comfortably."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"2D perceptron: it can shatter 3 non-collinear points (all 8 labelings are achievable by some line) but never 4 points (the XOR-like diagonal pattern is impossible), so d_vc = 3. General fact: a linear separator in d dimensions has d_vc = d + 1, the number of weights including the bias. Positive rays on the line (threshold classifiers h(x) = +1 for x >= a) shatter 1 point but not 2, so d_vc = 1. Rule of thumb: a model with d_vc = 5 wants about N = 50 examples for a trustworthy gap. Bound behavior: hold d_vc = 3 and grow N; the ln(N^3)/N inside the square root falls toward 0, so E_out closes in on E_in."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Define ''H shatters k points.'' 2) What is d_vc of a 2D perceptron? 3) What is d_vc of a linear separator in 10 dimensions (with bias)? 4) Using the 10 * d_vc rule, how many examples for d_vc = 4? 5) In the VC bound, does the penalty grow or shrink as d_vc increases? Answers: 1) for all 2^k labelings of those k points, some h in H realizes each one. 2) 3. 3) 10 + 1 = 11. 4) about 40. 5) it grows - higher complexity needs more data to generalize."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading d_vc as a count of parameters always - it usually tracks free parameters but is defined by shattering, not by counting. Mistake: claiming H shatters k points because it labels one arrangement correctly; you need all 2^k labelings. Exit: (a) What integer measures the complexity of H in the VC bound? (b) What is d_vc for a separating hyperplane in d dimensions? Answers: the VC dimension d_vc; d + 1."}]}'::jsonb, 'Notation: d_vc is the VC dimension - the most points H can shatter. ''Shatter'' means realize all 2^k labelings of k points. If H shatters some k points but no k+1 points, then d_vc = k.', 15),
  (8, 'The bias-variance tradeoff', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Split expected error into bias and variance and predict which way a model choice moves each. Warm-up: you fit the same target with a flat constant line versus a wiggly high-degree curve, each on many different small samples. Which one''s fits vary wildly from sample to sample?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Imagine training on many independent samples of size N, getting a different g each time. Average those fits into an average hypothesis g-bar. For a given input, expected squared error over samples splits into two parts. Bias is how far the average fit g-bar sits from the true target f - the error a model has even after averaging out sample luck, caused by H being too simple to represent f. Variance is how much individual fits g scatter around their own average g-bar - the error from chasing the particular sample. Simple models (few parameters, low d_vc) have high bias but low variance; complex models have low bias but high variance. Total error is their sum, so the best complexity is a compromise, not the extreme. Crucially, more data lowers variance, which lets you afford a more complex, lower-bias model."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Constant model g(x) = b: across samples the fitted constant barely moves (low variance) but a flat line cannot follow a sine target (high bias). A degree-10 polynomial on 12 points: it threads each sample almost perfectly, so g-bar can be close to f (low bias), but individual fits swing enormously between samples (high variance), and the swing dominates the error. Numbers: say a simple model has bias 0.20 and variance 0.05, total 0.25; a complex model has bias 0.02 and variance 0.40, total 0.42 - the simpler model wins here because data is scarce. Grow N so the complex model''s variance falls to 0.05: its total becomes 0.07 and it now wins. This is why the right model depends on how much data you have."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which term measures how far the average fit is from the target? 2) Which term measures sensitivity to the particular sample? 3) A very simple model tends to have high what? 4) A very complex model on little data tends to have high what? 5) If bias = 0.10 and variance = 0.15, what is the expected error (ignoring noise)? Answers: 1) bias. 2) variance. 3) bias. 4) variance. 5) 0.25."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the most flexible model is always best - on small data its variance dominates. Mistake: treating bias and variance as things you can both drive to zero at once with fixed data. Exit: (a) Total expected error is the sum of which two terms (plus noise)? (b) Which one does adding more data mainly reduce? Answers: bias and variance; variance."}]}'::jsonb, 'Decompose expected out-of-sample error as E_out = bias + variance (plus irreducible noise). Bias is error from a too-simple average fit; variance is error from sensitivity to the particular sample.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A hypothesis set shatters k points when it can realize...","options":["at least one labeling of them","all 2^k labelings of them","exactly half the labelings","the all-positive labeling only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shattering means every one of the 2^k plus/minus patterns is achievable by some h in H."},{"prompt":"The VC dimension of a separating hyperplane in d dimensions (with bias) is...","options":["d","d + 1","2d","d - 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It equals the number of free weights including the bias, d + 1. In 2D that is 3."},{"prompt":"In the VC generalization bound, increasing d_vc while holding N fixed makes the penalty term...","options":["smaller","larger","unchanged","negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Higher complexity raises the growth function and thus the bound''s penalty."},{"prompt":"Bias measures...","options":["how much fits scatter across samples","how far the average fit is from the target","the training sample size","the learning rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bias is the gap between g-bar and f; variance is the scatter of individual fits."},{"prompt":"A degree-10 polynomial fit on 12 points typically has...","options":["high bias, low variance","low bias, high variance","high bias, high variance","zero total error"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It fits each sample tightly (low bias) but swings wildly between samples (high variance)."},{"prompt":"Adding much more training data mainly reduces a model''s...","options":["bias","variance","number of features","learning rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"More data stabilizes the fit, shrinking variance and letting you afford lower bias."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Overfitting: fitting the noise', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize overfitting from E_in and E_out curves, and name its two drivers - too much complexity and too much noise. Warm-up: model A has E_in = 0.05, E_out = 0.30; model B has E_in = 0.10, E_out = 0.14. Which is overfitting, and which would you deploy?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Overfitting happens when a model reduces its in-sample error by fitting quirks of the particular sample - measurement noise, or accidental patterns - that do not hold in general. As you crank up complexity, E_in falls monotonically, but E_out follows a U-shape: it drops while added complexity captures real structure, then rises once the extra capacity is spent memorizing noise. The telltale sign is a widening gap between a still-falling E_in and a rising E_out. Two forces feed it. Stochastic noise is genuine randomness in the labels. Deterministic noise is target complexity that your H is too limited to capture, which acts just like noise relative to your model. Both make the sample misleading. Small N makes everything worse, since fewer points are easier to fit spuriously. The cure is not always a bigger model - often it is a simpler one, more data, or the regularization and validation tools coming next."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Two models: A (E_in 0.05, E_out 0.30) has a 0.25 gap - classic overfitting; B (E_in 0.10, E_out 0.14) generalizes far better and is the one to deploy, even though its training error is higher. Polynomial demo: fit noisy points sampled from a gentle degree-2 target. A degree-2 fit gets E_out about 0.05; a degree-9 fit drives E_in to 0 by snaking through every point but its E_out balloons to 0.8 as it invents wiggles between points. Even with no noise at all, if the true target is degree-50 and you fit degree-10, the unreachable complexity acts as deterministic noise and the higher-degree fit among your options can still overfit. Fewer points sharpen the trap: the same degree-9 fit overfits far worse on 10 points than on 100."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) During overfitting, which way does E_in move and which way does E_out move? 2) Is a low E_in always good news? 3) Name the two kinds of noise that drive overfitting. 4) Between model A (gap 0.25) and model B (gap 0.04), which is overfitting? 5) Does a smaller sample make overfitting more or less likely? Answers: 1) E_in keeps falling while E_out rises. 2) no - it can signal memorized noise if E_out is high. 3) stochastic noise and deterministic noise. 4) model A. 5) more likely."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: judging a model by E_in alone and picking the one that fits training best. Mistake: assuming overfitting requires noisy labels - deterministic noise from a too-simple H overfits even clean data. Exit: (a) What is the signature pattern of overfitting in the two error curves? (b) Name one cure other than shrinking the model. Answers: E_in falling while E_out rising (a widening gap); more data, regularization, or validation."}]}'::jsonb, 'Overfitting is not high error - it is a growing gap: E_in keeps dropping while E_out turns back up. The symptom is a model that looks better on training yet is worse on fresh data.', 14),
  (11, 'Regularization: reining in the fit', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how a weight penalty curbs overfitting and how the strength lambda moves bias and variance. Warm-up: if you force a fitted polynomial''s coefficients to stay small, will its curve be smoother or wigglier?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Regularization fights overfitting by adding a penalty to the quantity you minimize, so instead of just E_in you minimize E_aug = E_in + (lambda/N) w^T w. The term w^T w is the squared length of the weight vector; penalizing it pushes coefficients toward zero, which makes the fitted function smoother and less able to chase noise. This is weight decay, or ridge regression, and it is equivalent to constraining the weights to a ball of some radius. The knob lambda sets the strength. At lambda = 0 you get the raw overfitting-prone fit (low bias, high variance). As lambda grows, the fit stiffens: variance falls but bias rises, because you are forbidding the model from expressing sharp structure. So regularization slides you along the bias-variance tradeoff, and the goal is a middle lambda that minimizes E_out. Too much regularization underfits just as surely as too little overfits."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Suppose an unregularized degree-9 fit on noisy data gives E_in = 0.00 but E_out = 0.80 (overfit). Add weight decay with a small lambda: E_in rises slightly to 0.05 while E_out drops to 0.15 - a big win from a small penalty. Push lambda very large: the penalty dominates, weights are crushed toward 0, the fit flattens toward a constant, E_in climbs to 0.40 and E_out to 0.45 - now underfitting. A sweep might read: lambda = 0 gives E_out 0.80; lambda = 0.01 gives 0.15; lambda = 0.1 gives 0.12 (best); lambda = 1 gives 0.30; lambda = 10 gives 0.45. The U-shape in E_out versus lambda mirrors the U-shape in E_out versus complexity - regularization is complexity control by another route. You would pick lambda = 0.1 here, ideally chosen by validation."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the penalty term w^T w measure? 2) What happens to the fit as lambda increases toward large values? 3) Which error does regularization aim to reduce - E_in or E_out? 4) Does lambda = 0 regularize at all? 5) From the sweep (0.80, 0.15, 0.12, 0.30, 0.45 at lambda = 0, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10), which lambda is best? Answers: 1) the squared length of the weight vector. 2) it gets smoother and stiffer - variance down, bias up, eventually underfitting. 3) E_out. 4) no - it recovers the unregularized fit. 5) lambda = 0.1 (E_out 0.12)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming more regularization is always safer - too large a lambda underfits and raises E_out. Mistake: choosing lambda to minimize E_in, which just points back to lambda = 0. Exit: (a) What form does the standard weight-decay penalty take? (b) As lambda rises, which of bias and variance goes up and which goes down? Answers: (lambda/N) w^T w; bias goes up, variance goes down."}]}'::jsonb, 'Regularization adds a penalty on weight size to the error you minimize: E_aug = E_in + (lambda/N) w^T w. The strength lambda >= 0 trades training fit against smoothness; lambda = 0 recovers the unregularized fit.', 14),
  (12, 'Validation and cross-validation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Hold out a validation set to estimate E_out honestly, pick a model with it, and run K-fold cross-validation. Warm-up: you have 1000 labeled points and want to choose between 5 candidate values of lambda. Why can''t you use E_in to decide?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Because E_in is optimistic, you estimate E_out on data the training never saw. Split the sample: train on N - K points, hold out K points as a validation set, and the validation error E_val estimates E_out with a spread of about 1/sqrt(K). This gives an honest way to compare models: fit each candidate on the training part, score each on the validation part, keep the winner - then refit that winner on all the data. The tension is that a larger validation set K estimates E_out more reliably but starves training. Cross-validation resolves it: in K-fold, split the data into K equal folds, and for each fold train on the other K-1 folds and validate on the held-out fold, then average the K validation errors. Every point is used for validation exactly once and for training K-1 times, so you get a low-variance estimate of E_out without permanently sacrificing data. The extreme K = N is leave-one-out."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Holdout: from 1000 points, hold out 200, train each lambda on 800. Say E_val reads 0.22, 0.14, 0.13, 0.19, 0.25 for five lambdas - pick the third, then refit on all 1000. The estimate''s spread is about 1/sqrt(200), roughly 0.07, so nearby E_val values (0.14 versus 0.13) are within noise. 5-fold on 1000 points: five folds of 200; for lambda = 0.1 the five fold errors might be 0.12, 0.15, 0.11, 0.14, 0.13, averaging 0.13 - a steadier number than any single holdout. Do this for each candidate and pick the smallest average. Leave-one-out on N = 50: train 50 times on 49 points each, validate on the 1 left out, average the 50 one-point errors - most reliable, most expensive. Rule: never let a validation point influence training, or E_val stops estimating E_out."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why is E_in a poor basis for choosing lambda? 2) After using validation to pick the best model, what do you do next? 3) In K-fold cross-validation, how many times is each point used for validation? 4) What does raising the holdout size K do to the estimate and to training? 5) What is leave-one-out cross-validation? Answers: 1) it is optimistic - it was minimized on the same data, so it favors overfit models. 2) refit the chosen model on all the data. 3) exactly once. 4) it makes E_val more reliable but leaves less data to train on. 5) K-fold with K = N: each point is the sole validation point once while the other N - 1 train."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: tuning lambda on the validation set and then reporting that same E_val as the final performance - once used for a choice, it is contaminated; keep a separate untouched test set for the final number. Mistake: making the validation set so large that training collapses. Exit: (a) What does a validation set estimate that E_in cannot? (b) In K-fold, how many times is each point used for training? Answers: E_out; K - 1 times."}]}'::jsonb, 'A validation set is held out of training and used only to estimate E_out and to choose models or lambda. Once a point is used to make a choice, it is no longer a clean test point.', 14),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The signature of overfitting is that as complexity rises...","options":["both E_in and E_out fall","E_in falls while E_out rises","E_in rises while E_out falls","both stay constant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In-sample error keeps dropping while out-of-sample error turns back up - a widening gap."},{"prompt":"Deterministic noise refers to...","options":["random label errors","target structure too complex for H to capture","the learning rate","the size of the validation set"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Complexity your model cannot represent acts like noise relative to that model."},{"prompt":"The weight-decay penalty added to E_in is proportional to...","options":["w^T x","w^T w","1/N","E_out"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It penalizes the squared length of the weight vector, shrinking coefficients."},{"prompt":"As the regularization strength lambda increases, model variance...","options":["increases","decreases","stays fixed","becomes negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A stiffer fit scatters less across samples - variance falls while bias rises."},{"prompt":"A validation set gives an honest estimate of...","options":["E_in","E_out","the number of features","lambda"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Held-out data the model did not train on estimates out-of-sample error."},{"prompt":"In 5-fold cross-validation, each data point is used for validation...","options":["never","exactly once","five times","four times"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each point sits in exactly one held-out fold, and trains in the other four."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Gradient descent and logistic regression', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Run one step of gradient descent, choose a sane learning rate, and see how logistic regression turns a linear signal into a probability. Warm-up: to minimize a smooth error surface, should you step along the gradient or opposite to it?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Many models have no closed-form fit, so you minimize their error numerically. Gradient descent starts at some weights and repeatedly steps downhill: w <- w - eta * grad E(w), where grad E points in the direction of steepest increase, so its negative points downhill, and eta is the step size. Too small an eta crawls; too large an eta overshoots and can diverge - a moderate value that shrinks near the minimum works best. Stochastic gradient descent uses one random example''s gradient per step, which is cheap and noisy but scales to huge data. Logistic regression applies this to classification with confidence. Instead of a hard sign, it outputs theta(w^T x) = 1 / (1 + exp(-w^T x)), a number in (0, 1) read as the probability the label is +1. It is trained by minimizing the cross-entropy error, a smooth convex function, so gradient descent reliably finds the single best weights."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"One step in 1D: minimize E(w) = w^2, whose gradient is 2w. From w = 3 with eta = 0.1, w <- 3 - 0.1 * 6 = 2.4; again 2.4 - 0.1 * 4.8 = 1.92 - marching toward 0. Learning rate danger: same problem with eta = 1 gives w <- 3 - 1 * 6 = -3, then +3, then -3 - it oscillates and never settles; eta = 0.1 converges smoothly. Logistic output: with s = w^T x = 2, theta(2) = 1/(1 + exp(-2)) = 0.88, an 88 percent probability of the positive class; at s = 0, theta = 0.5 (maximum uncertainty); at s = -2, theta = 0.12. Cross-entropy rewards confident correct calls and heavily punishes confident wrong ones, which is why its gradient nudges weights toward well-calibrated probabilities."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the gradient-descent update rule. 2) From w = 4 on E(w) = w^2 with eta = 0.1, what is w after one step? 3) What goes wrong if eta is far too large? 4) What is theta(0) in logistic regression? 5) What does logistic regression output that the perceptron does not? Answers: 1) w <- w - eta * grad E(w). 2) 4 - 0.1 * 8 = 3.2. 3) the steps overshoot and can oscillate or diverge instead of converging. 4) 1/(1 + exp(0)) = 0.5. 5) a probability in (0, 1) rather than a hard plus/minus label."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: stepping in the +gradient direction, which climbs the error instead of descending. Mistake: fixing an eta so large the iterates blow up. Exit: (a) Which direction does gradient descent step? (b) What does the logistic function theta(s) return? Answers: opposite the gradient (downhill); a probability between 0 and 1."}]}'::jsonb, 'Gradient descent updates weights by w <- w - eta * grad, where eta is the learning rate (step size) and grad is the gradient of the error. Logistic regression outputs a probability theta(s) = 1 / (1 + exp(-s)) with s = w^T x.', 15),
  (15, 'Capstone: choosing a model honestly', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Assemble the whole course into an honest workflow that reports a trustworthy E_out, and spot the leaks that quietly inflate results. Warm-up: you try 20 models, pick the one with the best test error, and report that error as your final result. What is wrong?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every earlier lesson is a lever on the same quantity, E_out. The learning problem defines it; Hoeffding and the VC dimension bound it; the bias-variance split explains it; overfitting inflates it; regularization and validation control it; gradient descent minimizes E_in en route. An honest workflow chains them. First split off a test set and lock it away. Use the rest to train candidates and to run validation or cross-validation for every choice - which model family, which features, which lambda. Pick the winner by validation error, then refit it on all the non-test data. Only now, once, score it on the locked test set; that number is your reported E_out. The cardinal sin is data snooping: letting the test set influence any decision - even indirectly, by peeking at it to engineer features or by trying models until the test error looks good. Each such peek spends part of your generalization guarantee, exactly the union-bound cost from the second lesson."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Good workflow: 10000 points, hold out 2000 as a sealed test set. On the remaining 8000, use 5-fold cross-validation to choose among degrees 1 to 9 and lambdas 0, 0.01, 0.1, 1; the CV errors point to degree 3 with lambda = 0.1. Refit that single model on all 8000, then score the sealed 2000 once: E_out estimated at 0.11 - trustworthy. Snooping trap: instead you compute feature statistics on all 10000 points (test included) before splitting - the test set has leaked into the features, so its error now understates E_out. Multiple-testing trap: you try 20 models directly on the test set and report the best, 0.06; but the honest bound inflates that by the 20 tries, so the real E_out is worse - the winner won partly by luck. The fix in both cases is the same: decisions come from training and validation data only; the test set speaks once."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many times should the test set be used, and when? 2) After validation picks the best model, what do you do before the final test? 3) What is data snooping? 4) Why does trying many models on the test set and reporting the best understate E_out? 5) Which validation scheme reuses every point for both training and validation? Answers: 1) exactly once, at the very end. 2) refit the chosen model on all non-test data. 3) letting the test set influence any decision, directly or indirectly. 4) the best-of-many was partly luck; the union-bound cost inflates the true error above the reported minimum. 5) K-fold (or leave-one-out) cross-validation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: normalizing or selecting features using the full dataset before splitting - that leaks the test set. Mistake: iterating on the test set until the number looks good, which turns it into a training set. Exit: (a) What single discipline keeps a reported E_out honest? (b) What is the name for letting test data shape your choices? Answers: touch the test set only once, at the end; data snooping."}]}'::jsonb, 'The one rule that ties the course together: touch the test set exactly once, at the very end. Any data used to make a choice becomes training data and can no longer certify E_out.', 15),
  (16, 'Final quiz: learning from data', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The central quantity all of learning theory tries to control is...","options":["E_in","E_out","the learning rate eta","the number of features"],"answer":1,"explanation":"E_out, the out-of-sample error, is what generalization is about - see ''Capstone''."},{"prompt":"For a single fixed hypothesis, Hoeffding''s inequality guarantees that as N grows, E_in...","options":["diverges from E_out","becomes a reliable estimate of E_out","always equals zero","no longer matters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bad-event probability 2 exp(-2 eps^2 N) shrinks with N, pinning E_in to E_out."},{"prompt":"The VC dimension of a perceptron in the plane (2D, with bias) is...","options":["2","3","4","infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A line shatters 3 non-collinear points but never 4, so d_vc = 3 = d + 1."},{"prompt":"In the VC bound, the generalization penalty grows with d_vc and shrinks with...","options":["the learning rate","N, the sample size","lambda","the number of classes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"More data tightens the bound; more complexity loosens it."},{"prompt":"Expected out-of-sample error decomposes (ignoring noise) into...","options":["E_in plus E_val","bias plus variance","lambda plus eta","precision plus recall"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bias-variance decomposition splits expected error into bias and variance."},{"prompt":"A model with E_in = 0.02 and E_out = 0.40 is exhibiting...","options":["underfitting","overfitting","perfect generalization","high bias"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tiny training error with a large out-of-sample error is the overfitting signature."},{"prompt":"Weight-decay regularization minimizes E_in plus a term proportional to...","options":["w^T w","w^T x","E_out","1/N only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The penalty (lambda/N) w^T w shrinks the weights and smooths the fit."},{"prompt":"As the regularization strength lambda increases from 0, out-of-sample error typically...","options":["rises monotonically","falls monotonically","follows a U-shape with a best middle value","stays flat"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Too little overfits and too much underfits; a middle lambda minimizes E_out."},{"prompt":"In 5-fold cross-validation on N points, each fold used for validation has size about...","options":["N","N/5","5N","1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The data is split into 5 equal folds, so each holds about N/5 points."},{"prompt":"The gradient-descent update rule is...","options":["w <- w + eta * grad E","w <- w - eta * grad E","w <- eta * w","w <- grad E"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You step opposite the gradient (downhill) scaled by the learning rate eta."},{"prompt":"Logistic regression''s output theta(w^T x) is best interpreted as...","options":["a hard plus/minus label","a probability in (0, 1)","the raw signal s","the weight vector length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The logistic squashes s into (0, 1), read as the probability of the positive class."},{"prompt":"The core discipline for reporting an honest E_out is to...","options":["minimize E_in as hard as possible","use the test set only once, at the very end","try every model on the test set and pick the best","skip validation to save data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Any data used to make a choice becomes training data; the sealed test set speaks once - avoid data snooping."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f' and l.position = v.pos
);
